Network Models:
	- TCP/IP (4 layers) - Network interface, Internet, Host-to-Host Transport, Application
	- OSI (7 layers) - Physical, Data-Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application

	- Common Protocols for each layer
		- Application (FTP, SMTP, DNS, Telnet)
		- Host-to-Host (TCP, UDP)
		- Internet (IP, ARP, IGMP, ICMP)
		- Network (Ethernet, Token ring)


Network protocols:
	- IP (Internet Protocol) - addressing and communication between devices
	- TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) - focuses on transport of data packages
	- UDP (User Datagram Protocol) - like TCP but connectionless, no error checking
	- ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol) - Networking devices are using it (routers, ping is built on top of it)

Port numbers (/etc/services) - used to identify a network service (on top of TCP or UDP):
	- 0 - 1023 (system ports)
	- 1024 - 49151 (user ports)
	- 49152 - 65535 (private ports)

	- Default ports - 22/tcp SSH, 53/udp DNS, 80/tcp HTTP, 110/tcp POP3, 143/tcp IMAP

IP Protocol General Information:
	- IP Address (IPv4 and IPv6)
		- Static IP - it is manually entered to a config file
		- Dynamic IP - it is automatically assigned by the DHCP server and changes (usually when the device is restarted)

	- Network mask (subnet mask) - marks the boarder between two networks or subnets
	- Gateway address - connects two networks (in home networks it is the router)
	- Broadcast address - used for communication to all hosts on a network, it is the last address of a subnet


Network -> Subnet -> Hosts
		

